#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142589 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d93a0632-2268-50e0-8fd9-2f42f7822759" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers by Whole Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "54148f16-56b3-5ead-80fd-279c9b10ad29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13699" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $4 \\times 1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7}$, expressing the result as a mixed number." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4 = \\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 7) + 2}{7} = \\dfrac{9}{7}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1} \\times \\dfrac{9}{7} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 9}{1 \\times 7} = \\dfrac{36}{7}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{7} = 5\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 5\\,\\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142653 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ab483d4-57af-5b1d-845f-7be648396664" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Using a Strategy"}] :question/id #uuid "cba0777d-2ad9-5135-8b1f-bde135329d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14569" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $2\\,\\dfrac 2 {9} \\times 3$, expressing the result as a mixed number." :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 2 {9}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 9)+2}{9}= \\dfrac{20}{9}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\dfrac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{\\color{blue}9} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{blue}9$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{red}3$) have a common factor of $3$.\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{20}{9} \\cdot \\frac{3}{1} &= \\frac{20}{1} \\cdot \\frac{3}{9} \\\\\n&= 20 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 \\cdot 1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} =6\\,\\textrm{R} 2 = 6\\,\\dfrac 2 {3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142654 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d215ffbc-b13f-5168-85d0-c75612afa8a4" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where Simplification Is Needed"}] :question/id #uuid "2f9f1260-432a-56bc-b5e3-254b6f0fbad1" :question/math-academy-id "e-14570" :question/problem "Multiply $4 \\times 2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4 = \\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 4) + 1}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1} \\times \\dfrac{9}{4} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 9}{1 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{36}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{4} = \\dfrac{36 \\div 4}{4 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{9}{1} = 9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ab483d4-57af-5b1d-845f-7be648396664" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Using a Strategy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b86bfd33-540c-58c2-b38a-80cfd878137c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140794} {:answer/id #uuid "bc52b4cc-7496-5e3f-86e8-0f07e28bb64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140795} {:answer/id #uuid "17fb2243-ce09-559c-b76f-b9fe76d95ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140796} {:answer/id #uuid "30b78a2d-5907-51a4-a97c-e38620caec49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140797} {:answer/id #uuid "58f2025f-bb40-57f4-a0a7-b8d949abbf2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140798}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fde77874-765a-59c7-999a-80ef5d1e458c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140793}] :question/id #uuid "b7740ee8-6b81-5e7a-9844-9d92f4f6b3e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-57097" :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$3 \\cdot 1\\frac{2}{9} = \\frac{0}{3}$"}]}
